---------------------------------------- Charts in your app ------------------------------------------

- ADD A SCORE HISTORY PAGE AND RENDER IT WITH CHART.JS.

- Instructions: Angular Prerequisite "Advanced components" chapter

- Every serious business application includes a chart, and we aren’t going to be different!

1️⃣ Chart.js is a nice little charting library and we are going to add it to our application and
    see how to use it with Angular. We could use one of the libraries already ready for Angular,
    but the point is also to understand how these kinds of pure JavaScript libraries can interact
    with Angular.

    The idea is to add a new page in our application where we can follow the history of our score,
    as we lose some and win some on every bet.

    Let’s begin by adding chart.js to our app:

        npm install --save-exact chart.js@4.5.1
    
    We also need an adapter to let chart.js use date-fns to format the dates:

        npm install --save-exact chartjs-adapter-date-fns@3.0.0



2️⃣ Now, let’s create a new component called ScoreHistory:

        ng g c score-history
    


3️⃣ Add the proper configuration to app.routes.ts, so we can (lazily) navigate to this component
    with the path /score-history.

        { path: 'score-history', loadComponent: () => import('./score-history/score-history')
            .then(m => m.ScoreHistory)}



4️⃣ In our menu, wrap the span id="current-user" class="me-2" into an <a class="nav-link">, and add
    to it a routerLink to our new route. Replace the navbar-text class on the surrounding li with
    a nav-item class to restore the vertical alignement:

        <li class="nav-item">
            <a class="nav-link" routerLink="/score-history">
                <span id="current-user" class="me-2">
                {{ user.login }}
                <span class="fa fa-star"></span>
                {{ user.money | number }}
                </span>
            </a>
        </li>



5️⃣ The backend offers an API to fetch the score history
    (https://ponyracer.ninja-squad.com/apidoc#resources-money-history). Let’s add a
    getScoreHistory() method to our user service, that calls this API and returns the result. Add
    a new model in the models directory to represent the entity returned: ScoreHistoryModel, with
    two fields: instant and money.

        getScoreHistory(): Observable<ScoreHistoryModel[]>  {
            return this.http.get<ScoreHistoryModel[]>(`${environment.baseUrl}/api/money/history`);
        }
        //💡Observable<Array<ScoreHistoryModel>> and Observable<ScoreHistoryModel[]> are same thing



6️⃣ Now let’s go back to our component ScoreHistory. As you can see in the official Chart.js
    (https://www.chartjs.org/docs/latest/getting-started/) docs, we need to have a canvas element
    in our template. Let’s add one:
    score-history.html
        
        <div>
            <h1>Score history</h1>
            <canvas #chart></canvas>
        </div>



7️⃣ 🔥🔥Since the chart constructor needs a reference to the canvas DOM element, you’ll need to
    grab a reference to this element (name it canvas) and initialize the chart when this reference
    is available, with the data received from a resource calling the new getScoreHistory() method
    offered by our user service.

    To do so, create a history resource with rxResource, whose stream function simply returns
    this.userService.getScoreHistory(). 
    score-history.ts:

        protected readonly canvas = viewChild.required<ElementRef<HTMLCanvasElement>>('chart');
        private readonly history = rxResource({
            stream: () => this.userService.getScoreHistory()
        })



8️⃣ Then use an effect to create the chart once history.value() is available.
    To do so, you need to import Chart from chart.js, and call the constructor by giving it:
        👉 a context: in our case, it’s the nativeElement property of our canvas signal;
        👉 a configuration, with an attribute type, for us 'line' and an attribute data. data is
            an object composed of labels, in our case an array of the instants of the fetched
            history, and of datasets, an array of objects, that should look like:
        
    score-history.ts:

        constructor() {
            effect(onCleanup => {
                const history = this.history.value();
                if (!history) {
                    return;
                }
                const chart = new Chart(this.canvas().nativeElement, {
                    type: 'line',
                    data: {
                     👉 labels: history.map(event => event.instant),
                     👉 datasets: [
                            {
                                label: 'Score history',
                                backgroundColor: 'rgba(54, 162, 235, 0.2)',
                                borderColor: 'rgba(54, 162, 235, 1)',
                                fill: 'origin',
                                tension: 0.5,
                                data: history.map(event => event.money)
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    options: {
                        scales: {
                            x: {
                                type: 'time'
                            }
                        }
                    }
                });
                onCleanup(() => chart.destroy());
            });
        }

    The configuration accepts a third attribute options. To make our chart prettier, we can format
    the instants using date-fns (that Chart.js uses thanks to the adapter), and let Chart.js
    figure out what is the most comfortable unit to use based on the size of the scale.
    👉 Add an options attribute like this to indicate our x-axis is a time series:
    score-history.ts

        constructor() {
            effect(onCleanup => {
                const history = this.history.value();
                if (!history) {
                    return;
                }
                const chart = new Chart(this.canvas().nativeElement, {
                    type: 'line',
                    data: {
                        labels: history.map(event => event.instant),
                        datasets: [
                            {
                                label: 'Score history',
                                backgroundColor: 'rgba(54, 162, 235, 0.2)',
                                borderColor: 'rgba(54, 162, 235, 1)',
                                fill: 'origin',
                                tension: 0.5,
                                data: history.map(event => event.money)
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                 👉 options: {
                        scales: {
                            x: {
                                type: 'time'
                            }
                        }
                    }
                });
                onCleanup(() => chart.destroy());
            });
        }

    👉You also need to import the adapter:
    score-history.ts:

        import 'chartjs-adapter-date-fns';



9️⃣ 🔥Chart.js is doing its best to be tree-shakeable, so we need to manually register what parts of
    the library we want to use. Above the component, add:

        Chart.register(LineController, LinearScale, TimeScale, PointElement, LineElement, Legend,
            Filler, Tooltip);

    ✅That should do the trick! Try to bet several times if you didn’t already, and go to our new
    page to see your score history.



